libsanitizer: do not apply glibc's thread-descriptor size on musl mips

ThreadDescriptorSizeFallback() returns sizeof(struct pthread) values read off
particular glibc releases. Every other architecture reaches its value through
GetLibcVersion(), which answers false on a non-glibc libc and leaves the result
at 0; the mips arm returned its value unconditionally.

On musl that made GetTls() subtract a 1152-byte thread descriptor that is not
there, so LeakSanitizer was handed a TLS range beginning in unmapped memory and
its tracer died on the first byte: "Tracer caught signal 11: addr=...". The
aarch64 and powerpc64 arms have the same unguarded shape and would fail the
same way on a musl target; they are left alone here because there is no such
target to test on.

Signed-off-by: OpenIPC AI team <ai@openipc.org>

--- a/libsanitizer/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_linux_libcdep.cpp
+++ b/libsanitizer/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_linux_libcdep.cpp
@@ -297,7 +297,16 @@
   }
 #elif defined(__mips__)
   // TODO(sagarthakur): add more values as per different glibc versions.
-  val = FIRST_32_SECOND_64(1152, 1776);
+  // Only on glibc. Every value in this function is a sizeof(struct pthread)
+  // read off some glibc release, and the branches above reach them through
+  // GetLibcVersion(), which answers false on any other libc and leaves val at
+  // 0. mips reached its value unconditionally, so on musl GetTls() subtracted
+  // a 1152-byte thread descriptor that is not there and handed LeakSanitizer a
+  // TLS range starting in unmapped memory. The tracer then died reading its
+  // first byte -- "Tracer caught signal 11" -- which reads as a broken port
+  // rather than as one wrong constant.
+  if (SANITIZER_GLIBC)
+    val = FIRST_32_SECOND_64(1152, 1776);
 #elif SANITIZER_RISCV64
   int major;
   int minor;
